After teardown, accessing a member re-materializes it fresh. The whole
lifecycle, live:

<DemoTeardown />

### The `stopEffects()` hook — a reserved name

The naming pair carries the contract: you **call** `$stopEffects()` (engine
API, `$`-prefixed), and you **define** `stopEffects()` (your hook, plain
name). That makes `stopEffects` a reserved method name — any method called
that is invoked automatically during teardown, so never use the name for
anything else.

Three guarantees make the hook useful:

- **It runs first.** When your hook executes, nothing has been torn down
  yet — watchers are live and every cached ref still holds its value, so
  cleanup can read state (`this.socket.value.close()`).
- **It is for non-Vue resources.** Sockets, event listeners, timers,
  subscriptions from composables. Vue effects need no help — the scope
  stop right after the hook kills every `$watch`/`$watchEffect`.
- **A throwing hook cannot abort teardown.** The error propagates to the
  `$stopEffects()` caller, but the scope still stops and the caches still
  clear — a failed socket close can never leak an instance's watchers.

```ts
class $Session {
  get socket() {
    return ref<WebSocket | null>(null);
  }

  // the reserved hook — $stopEffects() calls this before anything else
  stopEffects() {
    this.socket.value?.close();
  }
}
```

