Now *that's* the right question — and reducing it honestly, I think you've spotted a genuine inconsistency in ivue's own philosophy. Let me lay out both sides, because there's one real argument each way:

**The case for removing the hook (your instinct):** The Standard's DO/NEVER table literally says: *NEVER "add an `init()` method expecting auto-call — ivue never calls it."* But `stopEffects()` is exactly that — an auto-called lifecycle method, just at the other end. The engine's cleanest possible invariant is **"ivue never calls your code, period"** — construction is your constructor, teardown is your explicit call. And the composition you describe is trivially expressible without any convention:

```ts
dispose() {
  this.socket.value?.close();
  this.$stopEffects();
}
```

Explicit, readable, greppable, no reserved name, no surprise — and the fact that *you*, the author, didn't fully know the hook existed is the empirical proof that magic auto-calls don't pay their documentation debt. Plus it deletes engine bytes (the hook call and part of the try/finally ladder).

**The one real counterargument:** `$stopEffects()` currently works as a *universal dispose protocol* — any generic owner (the `onScopeDispose(() => this.$stopEffects())` auto-wire bridge, a store manager disposing arbitrary instances) can tear down any instance *completely* without knowing its class. Remove the hook, and a class with non-Vue resources is only fully disposed via its own `dispose()` — generic callers of `$stopEffects` would silently leak the socket. My counter to the counter: ivue's own ownership doctrine says every outliving instance has an explicit owner, and the bridge is written *inside the class's constructor* — which can just as well call `this.dispose()`. The truly-generic-disposer scenario is hypothetical for a library that hasn't shipped v2 yet.

**My verdict: remove it.** The symmetry argument is decisive — it's your own stated invariant, and the hook violates it. "One way to do it" becomes: `$stopEffects()` resets the engine overlay, and anything more is an ordinary method you write and call like any other. Breaking-change cost is zero (v2 unpublished), and the docs I *just* wrote about the hook become obsolete — which is fine; better to delete fresh documentation than keep a wart because we documented it. Fittingly, those hook docs plus the mirror resplice are sitting **uncommitted** right now, so nothing needs unwinding.

Before I swing the axe: it touches the engine (`$stopEffects` implementation), the adversarial specs (two tests are about the hook), the Session template in the Standard, the lifecycle/API docs, and a check of the playground examples for hook usage. Confirm and I'll do the full sweep with all gates.

