My verdict: still no — and the reason got sharper now that I reduce it properly. The proposal sounds like one design, but it forks into two, and each fork inherits a flaw from the side it leans toward:

**Variant A — inject a default `stopEffects()`, user's definition shadows it.** The engine never auto-calls anything, good. But now the unified name has a weaker guarantee than even the old hook had: if a user writes `stopEffects()` and forgets to call `$stopEffects()` inside, the "generic" call silently leaks the scope. The one name every system relies on *sometimes doesn't tear down the engine*. A protocol that lies occasionally is worse than no protocol.

**Variant B — engine wraps/combines the user's `stopEffects` so engine teardown always runs.** That restores the guarantee, but it's the hook again wearing a different coat: reserved name, auto-invoked user code, ordering semantics, "what happens on double call" — everything we just deleted.

So the middle ground isn't a third clean point; it's a line between two clean points, and it bleeds from whichever end it's closer to.

There's also a structural cost both variants share: today the engine's namespace rule is airtight — **everything ivue injects starts with `$`; your flat namespace is untouched**. Injecting a bare `stopEffects` breaks that with exactly one exception, and one-exception rules are how APIs start rotting. It's also asymmetric with construction: we don't inject a generic `init()` — construction is your constructor. Teardown just became symmetric with that. This would un-symmetrize it.

And the payoff is small, because the unified API you want is a one-liner in userland, where it belongs:

```ts
class $Disposable {
  dispose() {
    this.$stopEffects();
  }
}
```

Every class in an app extends that (or defines its own richer `dispose()`), and now *that app* has the uniform `.dispose()` contract — with the name the app chose, at zero engine bytes. A truly generic owner that knows nothing about the app already has `$stopEffects()`, which is honestly all a stranger can safely do anyway. And if you want the platform-blessed version of "generic disposal protocol," that's literally what `Symbol.dispose` / `using` exists for — an app can add `[Symbol.dispose]() { this.dispose() }` to that same base class and get language-level support for free.

So: the engine ships the primitive, apps compose the protocol. Unified APIs are an application invariant, not an engine one — the engine forcing one name on everyone is the "too abstract" smell you felt, and I think your instinct was right the first time.

